      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2017 American College of Cardiology Foundation. Published by Elsevier Inc. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
            "content": "The authors sought to identify whether a coronary side branch (SB) is supplying a myocardial mass that may benefit from revascularization."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "The amount of subtending myocardium and physiological stenosis is frequently different between the main vessel (MV) and SB."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "In this multicenter registry, 482 patients who underwent coronary computed tomography angiography and fractional flow reserve (FFR) measurement were enrolled. The % fractional myocardial mass (FMM), the ratio of vessel-specific myocardial mass to whole myocardium, was assessed in 5,860 MV or SB consisting of 2,930 bifurcations. Physiological stenosis was defined by fractional flow reserve (FFR) <0.80. Myocardial mass that may benefit from revascularization was defined by %FMM ≥10%."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "In per-bifurcation analysis, MV supplied a 1.5- to 9-fold larger myocardial mass compared with SB. Unlike left main bifurcation (n = 482), only 1 of every 5 non-left main SB (n = 2,448) supplied %FMM ≥10% (97% vs. 21%; p < 0.001). SB length ≥73 mm could estimate %FMM ≥10% (c-statistic = 0.85; p < 0.001). In 604 vessels interrogated by FFR, diameter stenosis was similar (p = NS), but %FMM ≥10%, FMM/minimal luminal diameter, and frequency of FFR <0.80 was higher in MV compared with SB (p < 0.001, all). Generalized estimating equations modeling demonstrate that vessel diameter, left myocardial mass, and FFR were not (p = NS), but SB length ≥73 mm and left main bifurcation were significant predictors for %FMM ≥10% (p < 0.001)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "Compared with MV, SB supplies a smaller myocardial mass and showed less physiological severity despite similar stenosis severity. SB supplying a myocardial mass of %FMM≥10%, which may benefit revascularization could be identified by vessel length ≥73 mm. Pre-procedural recognition of these findings may guide optimal revascularization strategy for bifurcation."
          }
        ]
      },
